ow take $y = x/n$, multiply by $\\Lambda(n)$ and sum over $n$; on $\\Re s = 2$ the series $\\sum_n \\Lambda(n)n^{-s} = -\\frac{\\zeta''}{\\zeta}(s)$ converges absolutely, so\n\\[ \\sum_{n \\le x} \\Lambda(n)\\Bigl(\\frac xn - 1\\Bigr) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\int_{2 - i\\infty}^{2 + i\\infty} -\\frac{\\zeta''}{\\zeta}(s)\\,\\frac{x^s}{s(s-1)}\\,ds. \\]\nMoving the line of integration to the left and collecting residues gives, for $x \\ge 1$,\n\\[ G_{\\mathbf 1}(x) = \\log 2\\pi - \\sum_\\rho \\frac{x^\\rho}{\\rho(\\rho - 1)} - \\sum_{n \\ge 1} \\frac{x^{-2n}}{2n(2n+1)}, \\]\nwhere $\\rho$ runs over the nontrivial zeros with multiplicity and the sum converges absolutely \\cite{Davenport}. The residue at $s = 1$ is the main term $x\\log x - (1+\\gamma)x$, which $G_{\\mathbf 1}$ subtracts; the three terms that remain come from $s = 0$ (where $\\frac{\\zeta''}{\\zeta}(0) = \\log 2\\pi$), from the zeros $\\rho$, and from the trivial zeros $-2n$. So each zero $\\rho = \\beta + i\\gamma$ adds an oscillation $x^\\rho = x^\\beta e^{i\\gamma\\log x}$ of size $x^\\beta$.\n\n\\emph{Taylor coefficients.} Since $\\int_1^\\infty x^\\rho\\, x^{-s-1}\\,dx = \\frac{1}{s - \\rho}$ for $\\Re s > \\Re\\rho$, the \\hyperref[8JS2]{Mellin transform} of the explicit formula is\n\\begin{align*}\nH_{\\mathbf 1}(s) = \\frac{\\log 2\\pi}{s} &- \\sum_\\rho \\frac{1}{\\rho(\\rho-1)(s - \\rho)} \\\\\n&- \\sum_{n \\ge 1} \\frac{1}{2n(2n+1)(s + 2n)}.\n\\end{align*}\nHence, for $\\Re z > 1$ and $k \\ge 0$, the coefficients $A_k(z) = \\frac{(-1)^k}{k!}H_{\\mathbf 1}^{(k)}(z)$ of the \\hyperref[8JT2]{one-sided bound} are\n\\begin{align*}\nA_k(z) = \\frac{\\log 2\\pi}{z^{k+1}} &- \\sum_\\rho \\frac{1}{\\rho(\\rho-1)(z - \\rho)^{k+1}} \\\\\n&- \\sum_{n \\ge 1} \\frac{1}{2n(2n+1)(z + 2n)^{k+1}}.\n\\end{align*}\nEach pole $p$ of $H_{\\mathbf 1}$, with residue $c$, contributes $c/(z - p)^{k+1}$, so for large $k$ the poles nearest to $z$ dominate. This is the mechanism behind the \\hyperref[8JT2]{one-sided bound} and the \\hyperref[8JT5]{lower bound}.\n\n\\emph{What the theorem says here.} For $\\psi = \\mathbf 1$ the \\hyperref[8JK2]{interval estimate} only asks that $G_{\\mathbf 1}(x) \\le x^{1/2 + 9\\varepsilon}$ on sparse intervals $[Y, Y^{3/2}]$, and \\ref{8JR6} turns this into the Riemann hypothesis. Conversely, if the Riemann hypothesis holds, then $|x^\\rho| = \\sqrt x$ and $\\rho(1 - \\rho) = |\\rho|^2$ for every zero, and $\\sum_\\rho \\frac{1}{\\rho(1-\\rho)} = 2 + \\gamma - \\log 4\\pi \\approx 0.0462$ \\cite{Davenport}. With $\\sum_{n \\ge 1} \\frac{1}{2n(2n+1)} = 1 - \\log 2 < 0.307$, the explicit formula then gives the much stronger two-sided bound\n\\[ |G_{\\mathbf 1}(x) - \\log 2\\pi| \\le 0.0462\\sqrt x + 0.307 \\qquad (x \\ge 1). \\]\n\n\\emph{Graphs.} Panel (a) shows $G_{\\mathbf 1}(x)$ for $1 \\le x \\le 10^7$, on a logarithmic $x$-axis, with this bound dashed.\n\\begin{center}\n\\textbf{(a)} $G_{\\mathbf 1}(x)$ and the bound $\\log 2\\pi \\pm (0.0462\\sqrt x + 0.307)$, for $1 \\le x \\le 10^7$\n\\end{center}\n\\includegraphics[width=1, alt=G_1(x) for x from 1 to 10 million on a logarithmic axis, oscillating with amplitude up to about 45, well inside the dashed curves log 2 pi plus or minus (0.0462 sqrt x + 0.307)]{/static/figures/zeta-smoothed-sum.svg}\nPanel (b) shows $(G_{\\mathbf 1}(x) - \\log 2\\pi)/\\sqrt x$ for $10 \\le x \\le 10^7$, together with the explicit formula cut off after the first zero pair $\\frac12 \\pm 14.1347\\,i$, which is a pure wave in $\\log x$, and after the first ten zero pairs, which already follows the data closely.\n\\begin{center}\n\\textbf{(b)} $\\bigl(G_{\\mathbf 1}(x) - \\log 2\\pi\\bigr)/\\sqrt x$ and the explicit formula with the first one and ten zero pairs, for $10 \\le x \\le 10^7$\n\\end{center}\n\\includegraphics[width=1, alt=The normalized smoothed sum oscillating with size about 0.01 to 0.02, the first zero pair giving a pure wave and the first ten zero pairs following the data closely]{/static/figures/zeta-